<p>
The next stop was the floating market.  For over a hundred years, merchants have been selling their goods on boats to other people via boats.  All of your business was done on the water in this famous canal.  Mand I did our wheeling and dealing and got some good bargains.  We found out later they were good because the same items sold by shopkeepers whose stores line the canal charged more.  We attribute this to the overhead of having a shop.  If you didn't like the price, you were encouraged to bargain down by the merchants themselves.  This is how you do business in these parts.  You never take the first price you are told.  You are expected to bargain and if look or even touch one item, they hound you until you make an offer.  When that happened to us by mistake (i.e. we had no interest in the item), we would just bargain some ridiculously low price and we knew they would turn us away.
<p>
