<p>
The next stop would be the Samphram Elephant Grounds and Crocodile Farm.  It was about 45 min away and involved a peaceful ride thrown the countryside where you could see many rectangular man-made ponds containing fish (i.e. fish farms) and salt farms.  Salt was filtered from salt water and further refined in factories so it could be fit for human consumption.  When we got to the Elephant Grounds, we had lunch and then watched the elephant show.  Elephants and their trainers or manhouts havea a strong bond.  It's almost as if they aremarried.  Some bonds exist for 20+years.  I remember one particular story where the manhout tied up his elephant to a pole and went into town to go to a brothel. The elephant got mad, tore up the pole, went in the direction of the manhout and destroyed the property of the local city people.  The city locals were mad and give the bill of damages to the manhout.  The lesson there is never cheat on your elephant or it will cost you big.  Anyway, they showed a demonstration of how elephants were used in Thailand from work like picking up and dragging logs to hunting, to circus performers, and finally for battle. 
<p>
Following the show was the crocodile show.  A pair of trainers did crazy things with the crocs.  There were probably 6-10 crocs in a moat around a cement floor.  The trainers would drag up the crocs by their tails onto the cement floor and proceeded to tease them by sticking body parts and even their heads into the mouths of the crocs right before the mouths would snap shut.  Crazy if you ask me!  
<p>
The final show of the day was another Thai dance show at a place called the Rose Garden, which by the way did not have a single rose on the premises.  I would have yawned at the show if it was only dancing, but it was so much more. They had exhibitions of Muay Thai kickboxing, swordfighting, and even a wedding ceremony.  Very informative.  Ah, the day was coming to a close, except we got dragged to the same jeweller stone again as the day before.  This is when M and I realized that they must be giving kickbacks to the tour guides for bring tourists over there. 
